<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Logroño</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Logroño is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the capital of the autonomous community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rioja">La Rioja</a>. Near the Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vareia">Vareia</a>, it grew in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> as a crossing point on the pilgrim route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, and received a charter in 1095. Long a frontier town between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, it is now the centre of the Rioja wine trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/logrono.jpg" alt="Logroño" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Jones from Kelowna, BC, Canada · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Town_Plaza_-_Logrono_-_La_Rioja_-_Spain_(14576256386)_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
